{"id":"scheduling-eviction-taint-and-toleration","corpus":"k8s","title":"Taints and Tolerations","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Taints and Tolerations"},{"kind":"p","text":"Node affinity is a property of Pods that attracts them to a set of nodes (either as a preference or a hard requirement). Taints are the opposite -- they allow a node to repel a set of pods."},{"kind":"p","text":"Tolerations are applied to pods. Tolerations allow the scheduler to schedule pods with matching taints. Tolerations allow scheduling but don't guarantee scheduling: the scheduler also evaluates other parameters as part of its function."},{"kind":"p","text":"Taints and tolerations work together to ensure that pods are not scheduled onto inappropriate nodes. One or more taints are applied to a node; this marks that the node should not accept any pods that do not tolerate the taints."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Concepts"},{"kind":"p","text":"You add a taint to a node using kubectl taint. For example,"},{"kind":"p","text":"places a taint on node node1. The taint has key key1, value value1, and taint effect NoSchedule. This means that no pod will be able to schedule onto node1 unless it has a matching toleration."},{"kind":"p","text":"To remove the taint added by the command above, you can run:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You specify a toleration for a pod in the PodSpec. Both of the following tolerations \"match\" the taint created by the kubectl taint line above, and thus a pod with either toleration would be able to schedule onto node1:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The default Kubernetes scheduler takes taints and tolerations into account when selecting a node to run a particular Pod. However, if you manually specify the .spec.nodeName for a Pod, that action bypasses the scheduler; the Pod is then bound onto the node where you assigned it, even if there are NoSchedule taints on that node that you selected. If this happens and the node also has a NoExecute taint set, the kubelet will eject the Pod unless there is an appropriate tolerance set."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's an example of a pod that has some tolerations defined:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The default value for operator is Equal."},{"kind":"p","text":"A toleration \"matches\" a taint if the keys are the same and the effects are the same, and:"},{"kind":"li","text":"the operator is Exists (in which case no value should be specified), or"},{"kind":"li","text":"the operator is Equal and the values should be equal."},{"kind":"p","text":"There are two special cases:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If the key is empty, then the operator must be Exists, which matches all keys and values. Note that the effect still needs to be matched at the same time."},{"kind":"p","text":"An empty effect matches all effects with key key1."},{"kind":"p","text":"The above example used the effect of NoSchedule. Alternatively, you can use the effect of PreferNoSchedule."},{"kind":"p","text":"The allowed values for the effect field are:"},{"kind":"p","text":"NoExecute"},{"kind":"p","text":"This affects pods that are already running on the node as follows:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pods that do not tolerate the taint are evicted immediately"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pods that tolerate the taint without specifying tolerationSeconds in their toleration specification remain bound forever"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pods that tolerate the taint with a specified tolerationSeconds remain bound for the specified amount of time. After that time elapses, the node lifecycle controller evicts the Pods from the node."},{"kind":"p","text":"NoSchedule"},{"kind":"p","text":"No new Pods will be scheduled on the tainted node unless they have a matching toleration. Pods currently running on the node are not evicted."},{"kind":"p","text":"PreferNoSchedule"},{"kind":"p","text":"PreferNoSchedule is a \"preference\" or \"soft\" version of NoSchedule. The control plane will try to avoid placing a Pod that does not tolerate the taint on the node, but it is not guaranteed."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can put multiple taints on the same node and multiple tolerations on the same pod. The way Kubernetes processes multiple taints and tolerations is like a filter: start with all of a node's taints, then ignore the ones for which the pod has a matching toleration; the remaining un-ignored taints have the indicated effects on the pod. In particular,"},{"kind":"li","text":"if there is at least one un-ignored taint with effect NoSchedule then Kubernetes will not schedule the pod onto that node"},{"kind":"li","text":"if there is no un-ignored taint with effect NoSchedule but there is at least one un-ignored taint with effect PreferNoSchedule then Kubernetes will try to not schedule the pod onto the node"},{"kind":"li","text":"if there is at least one un-ignored taint with effect NoExecute then the pod will be evicted from the node (if it is already running on the node), and will not be scheduled onto the node (if it is not yet running on the node)."}],"lemmas":{"taints":["taint","taints"],"tolerations":["toleration","tolerations"],"affinities":["affinity","affinities"],"properties":["property","properties"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"attracts":["attracts","attract"],"attracted":["attracted","attract"],"attracting":["attracting","attract"],"sets":["set","sets"],"setting":["setting","set"],"nodes":["node","nod","nodes"],"nods":["nod","nods"],"nodded":["nodded","nod"],"nodding":["nodding","nod"],"eithers":["eithers","either"],"preferences":["preference","preferences"],"hards":["hards","hard"],"harder":["harder","hard"],"requirements":["requirement","requirements"],"opposites":["opposite","opposites"],"allows":["allows","allow"],"allowing":["allowing","allow"],"repels":["repels","repel"],"applied":["applied","apply"],"applieds":["applied